{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261110T135352Z-28ab4045", "ts": "2026-11-10T13:56:36Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.1-dev", "branch": "feature/retire-clear-verdict", "build": "52bb14851a37"}, "body": {"base_commit": "4b6c7129068bbf9cc60eda83291ef169e2c45e5d", "base_tree": "b1f52ef5eb55ab041ed10f4d20cd55e5a2988b4e", "head_tree": "91191f74d56e7a5f4020bbb4d53210b2c5864fd5", "head_commit": "8efcb931c43d996e8186c456e091866117087245", "dispatch_commit": "8efcb931c43d996e8186c456e091866117087245", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/hooks/gates.json", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/stats.py", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "tests/test_stats.py", "tests/test_stop_block_facts.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-retire-clear-verdict.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/digest-behavior-inventory.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/changes/retire-clear-verdict.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/hooks/gates.json", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/stats.py", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "tests/test_stats.py", "tests/test_stop_block_facts.py", "tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-retire-clear-verdict.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/digest-behavior-inventory.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/changes/retire-clear-verdict.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "session-hygiene.md still says the Stop hook refuses COMPLETE/YOUR TURN over DO NOT CLEAR", "recommendation": "Chunk 01's description is to 'update every surface that says it refuses', and the paragraph at line 39 was updated. But the 'Five ways to get the label wrong' list in the same file (plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md:62) still ends its fourth item with \"`COMPLETE` over `DO NOT CLEAR`, or `YOUR TURN` over it with nothing a clear would kill, which the Stop hook refuses\". That is now false, and it contradicts line 39 in the same guide. Core rule: 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees: search for the claim, not the tokens you edited'. Fix it by dropping or rewording the clause, for example to 'which the Stop hook records'. Re-measure the session-hygiene.md token reading in tests/test_v5_methodology.py after the edit. Consider registering the retired wording in project-state's retired-claims list so `retired-claim-survives` owns this claim from now on. Sibling stale docstrings outside the subject set say 'clear-verdict gate': plugin/lib/gates.py:34 and plugin/lib/standing_block.py:75,80.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "stats --json default shape gains stop_observations without moving REPORT_SCHEMA_VERSION", "recommendation": "Every by_version bucket now carries a new `stop_observations` key, but stats.REPORT_SCHEMA_VERSION stays at 2. Precedent in this file goes the other way. 25c4e911 bumped 1->2 when it added keys to stop_blocks. The --by-build commit (6e901ff9) explicitly kept the key out of the default shape 'so ... REPORT_SCHEMA_VERSION does not move'. The one in-repo consumer (lib/contribution.py) indexes by key, so nothing breaks today. Either bump to 3 and add stop_observations to the --json key list in governance-telemetry.md, or record why an additive key leaves the version where it is.", "files": ["plugin/lib/stats.py", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Out-of-diff descriptions still say clear-verdict blocks or refuses", "recommendation": "Why it broke: the sweep updated the files the change edited, but the claim 'clear-verdict blocks' also lives in the readers of the detector. Survivors: plugin/lib/gates.py turn_declares_in_flight docstring ('The Stop hook refuses ``COMPLETE`` with ``DO NOT CLEAR`` outright, and ``YOUR TURN`` with it unless...'), turn_contradicts_its_verdict ('returns ``None``, because the caller blocks on it') and the module docstring ('the Stop deferral and clear-verdict gate read'); plugin/lib/standing_block.py contradiction() ('because the caller blocks on what this returns and a block on an ambiguous turn would be a false one') and handed_over_reason / CLEAR_VERDICT_REASONS ('the clear-verdict gate'); plugin/bin/prawduct-hook STOP_BLOCK_ANSWER comment ('an item faults the close itself (the clear-verdict and clear-reason gates)'), although clear-verdict can no longer be an item in a block. Consequences: someone reading turn_declares_in_flight decides that YOUR TURN/COMPLETE + DO NOT CLEAR is already refused, when nothing now blocks it. Someone tuning contradiction()'s precision weighs a false-block cost that is gone, when the real tradeoff now is undercounting versus overcounting observations. This also misses the plan's own deliverable to update 'every surface that says it refuses'. Core learning: 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees: search for the claim, not the tokens you edited'. Scope: class. Search plugin/ for 'clear-verdict gate', 'refuses', 'caller blocks' and 'outright' near the clear verdict, and expect hits outside the diff. Close it by construction, not with a longer list: record the retirement as a decision with `retires:` phrases (e.g. \"clear-verdict gate\", \"refuses `COMPLETE`\") so the retired-claim-survives check sweeps the repo, then fix or delete each hit.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/standing_block.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "New shipped prose narrates retirement history and anchors to a work-cycle name", "recommendation": "Deletion, not rewording. Three places carry retirement history. The cmd_stop comment in plugin/bin/prawduct-hook says 'The check blocked from 3.7.0 until the owner retired it on 2026-11-10 (#1044)'. governance-telemetry.md says 'Since `retire-clear-verdict` its firings are observations', which anchors meaning to a work-cycle name, and also gives the 3.7.0..2026-11-10 history. A test class docstring cites '(owner, 2026-11-10, #1044)'. That history belongs to the change fragment, which already has it. Keep only the present-tense rule: the check observes and does not block, and it is scored by the same verdict rule. While there, change 'Neither is read by any gate' in governance-telemetry.md, which now follows three writers, to 'None'. Scope: instance. This is prose that nothing reads, so no extra commit is owed beyond folding it in.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "tests/test_stop_block_facts.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "append_stop_observation says its Stop was not blocked, but another gate may block it", "recommendation": "evidence.append_stop_observation's docstring says 'The Stop it fired on was not blocked, so there is no ``stop`` id or ``co_gates``'. The STOP_OBSERVED_PREFIX comment says the same ('a Stop that was let through'). cmd_stop records the observation before the other gates run, so the same Stop can still be blocked by reflection, critic or clear-reason. When that happens the observation has no link to the block's facts: before this change a reflection+clear-verdict Stop shared a `stop` id, and now 'fires together' can no longer be queried for this check. Either make the claim relational ('this check did not block the Stop'), or, if co-occurrence with blocks is worth keeping, record the observation after the blocker list is final and pass the Stop's id and co_gates through. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "Retired 'clear-verdict gate refuses' wording survives in the changed test module and three unchanged sites", "recommendation": "Learnings cross-check escalation. The rule 'A docstring or comment is a claim: ... after a reversal, port or disabled wiring, re-grep your own prose for the old world' (hook-surface.md), and core's 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees', both describe this change. It reverses a block, and the prose describing the old behaviour was swept only where the diff already touched it. The module docstring of tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py (lines 12-17, a subject file) still says COMPLETE + DO NOT CLEAR 'defers nothing and the clear-verdict gate refuses it. So does YOUR TURN ...'. The class docstring 530 lines further down states the opposite contract. The same old-world wording also survives at plugin/lib/standing_block.py:75 and :80 ('the clear-verdict gate records'), plugin/lib/gates.py:34 ('the Stop deferral and clear-verdict gate read') and tests/test_critic_session_guard.py:1468 ('the clear-verdict gate lets YOUR TURN + DO NOT CLEAR stand'). The consequence: a maintainer who reads the test file's contract from its header is told the hook refuses a turn that it now lets through. Scope: class. Fix the survivors (search `clear-verdict gate` and `refuses` near `clear-verdict` across plugin/ and tests/). Then turn the sweep into a construction instead of a longer list: add a `Retires:` field to the resolved clear-verdict entry in nonfunctional-requirements.md `## Direction`, quoting e.g. \"clear-verdict gate refuses\" and \"clear-verdict gate lets\". `retired-claim-survives` (lib/retired_claims.py) then owns every future survivor. This change declared no Retires phrase, so that check had nothing to look for.", "files": ["tests/test_stop_verdict_defer.py", "plugin/lib/standing_block.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "tests/test_critic_session_guard.py", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Shipped hook comment narrates the check's history (version, date, issue id)", "recommendation": "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook, the comment above `_record_stop_observation(... \"clear-verdict\" ...)` in cmd_stop, opens with 'The check blocked from 3.7.0 until the owner retired it on 2026-11-10 (#1044)'. Review protocol, Prose remedies: review history never belongs in a shipped comment, and one that narrates history is a deletion finding. Delete the history clause and keep the rationale: the harm is a session kept open needlessly, firings are still recorded so stats can score them. The history already lives in the change fragment and the NFR ruling. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner (one marked inferred)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Goals trace to the owner ruling of 2026-11-10 on #1044 ('retire clear-verdict but keep measurement'), and the NFR Direction entry was rewritten as a resolved exception citing that ruling, so the decision has a recorded why. The plan lists three mid-build decisions: (1) clear-verdict leaves hooks/gates.json, because the registry lists blocking gates; (2) the hook prints nothing on a firing, because output on a passing Stop would teach the model to ignore the hook (marked *Inferred*, so it is the one the owner has not confirmed); (3) pre-retirement stop-gate:clear-verdict facts are left in place and read as before. There was no alignment-pass miss. The downstream contract change is additive: a new `stop_observations` key in stats JSON, and the registry row removed. The only registry readers are the hook's attribution and the new-gate banner, and neither needs a row for a check that no longer blocks.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-retire-clear-verdict.md"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check: the retirement rationale cites a measurement without its numbers or command", "recommendation": "authoring.md: 'Rewriting a MEASUREMENT into a BENEFIT widens the claim \u2014 re-attach the number'. Also 'Never write a present-tense state claim ... write the dated measurement plus the command'. The NFR ruling and the change fragment, which ships to the CHANGELOG, say 3.8 'scored about as often wrong as right'. The claim is accurate. The figures behind it (3.8: 7 catch / 9 slip across testrouter, discodon and prawduct) live only in the ephemeral .handoff-notes.md. If you touch the NFR entry for the Retires field above anyway, attach the figure and `prawduct-hook stats` as its source. No standalone edit is wanted. Scope: instance.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/changes/retire-clear-verdict.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: #932 stop-gate: the clear-verdict block fires on YOUR TURN + DO NOT CLEAR", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The block no longer exists, so a block firing on YOUR TURN + DO NOT CLEAR is moot. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update 932 status=shipped closed-by=scope:retire-clear-verdict` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says.", "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears partly resolved: #1044 governance: decide if clear-verdict and clear-reason earn their cost", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The clear-verdict half is decided and shipped here. The NFR keeps clear-reason as 'conforms' with a named expected yield, but the plan records no owner ruling on clear-reason. Before marking it shipped (`/prawduct:backlog update 1044 status=shipped closed-by=scope:retire-clear-verdict`), confirm that the clear-reason half is settled, or record that it stays open.", "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog hygiene (C-B3): open #928's requirements inventory still lists clear-verdict as a blocking gate", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. documentation/issues/928-requirements.md rows 4, 8 and 39 list clear-verdict among the Stop gates (row 8: 'gate (clear-verdict, clear-reason)'). #928 is an open item in an area this diff touches, and no chunk updated it. When #928 is next worked, reclassify clear-verdict as a non-blocking observation.", "files": ["documentation/issues/928-requirements.md"], "fid": "R-12"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 3, "note": 9}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "retire-clear-verdict", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01"], "chunks_proposed": ["01"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "4b6c7129068bbf9cc60eda83291ef169e2c45e5d", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-retire-clear-verdict.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/digest-behavior-inventory.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/changes/retire-clear-verdict.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-retire-clear-verdict.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "lea